  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 318 PM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nDry conditions are expected to persist into tomorrow as a \nsprawling ~1025 mb surface high continues to exert its influence\nacross the region. The surface pressure gradient will tighten \nas the center of this surface high repositions itself to our \neast and a ~996 mb surface low tracks from northern Manitoba \ninto northern Ontario with an associated cold front pressing \nsoutheastward towards the Great Lakes. This will yield breezy \nsouthwesterly flow here tomorrow -- primarily across the \nnorthern half of our forecast area, where some gusts into the \n25-30 mph range are likely during the afternoon. The same dry \nair mass that has allowed for air temperatures to overperform \nmost raw model guidance today will remain in place for tomorrow,\nsuggesting that a similar theme of overperforming temperatures \nwill be followed for yet another day. Accordingly, our gridded \nforecast for tomorrow contains high temperatures in the low-mid\n70s that fall in the upper echelon of available forecast \nguidance.\n\nThe aforementioned cold front will track across our forecast \narea early Wednesday, though it won't really seem like much of a\ncold frontal passage as relatively deep post-frontal mixing \ninto drier air aloft should largely offset the modest post-\nfrontal cold air advection. When coupled with mostly clear \nskies, high temperatures on Wednesday actually look like they \nwill be slightly warmer then Tuesday's highs and could even \nreach the low 80s across our southern CWA, in spite of the \nfrontal passage. Cooler high temperatures in the mid 60s to mid \n70s are ultimately expected on Thursday as modest northwesterly \nto northerly winds persist and the heart of the cooler air mass \nsettles into the region. Similar temperature readings are then \nfavored again on Friday before strengthening south to \nsouthwesterly low-level flow helps facilitate another warm-up \ninto this weekend.\n\nWith a dearth of moisture accompanying Wednesday's frontal \npassage and surface high pressure settling back into the region \nthereafter, no precipitation is expected on Wednesday or \nThursday. There remains a signal in ensemble guidance for some \ncombination of a low-amplitude shortwave, isentropic ascent, \nweak frontogenesis, and somewhat steepened mid-level lapse rates\nto come together and induce a wave of showers or sprinkles \nsomewhere in the region sometime in the Thursday night into \nFriday time frame. However, there isn't much spatiotemporal \nconsistency with this at this time, and even if things were to \ncome together just right to support precipitation in our \nforecast area during this time frame, the persisting parched \nlow-mid-level air mass would likely play some role in limiting \nthe amount and/or the spatial footprint of any measurable \nprecipitation.\n\nEnsemble guidance then supports precipitation-free conditions \nacross our area on Saturday and mostly favors those conditions \ncontinuing into Sunday at this time. Will note that there is \nsome potential for deeper moisture associated with a tropical \ndisturbance currently over the Bay of Campeche to be approaching\nour latitude come Sunday, though there is still a high degree \nof uncertainty at this point with how that tropical disturbance \nwill evolve and whether its associated moisture will eventually \nbe advected in the general direction of the Great Lakes or be \npulled more northeastward towards the Mid-Atlantic.\n\nOgorek",
